//Access Specifiers

Access specifiers control who can access the members of a class.

There are 3:

1. public

Can be accessed from anywhere.

class Student {
public:
    int age;
};

Student s;
s.age = 20;

2. private

Can be accessed only inside the class.

class Student {
private:
    int age;
};

This won't work:

Student s;
s.age = 20;  // ❌

3. protected

Can be accessed:

inside the class
by derived/child classes

We'll understand protected properly when we study Inheritance.

Simple way to remember
Specifier	Outside class	Child class	 Inside class
public	          ✅	           ✅	       ✅
private	          ❌	           ❌	       ✅
protected	      ❌	           ✅	       ✅

That's it for Access Specifiers.

1. Inside the class

Code written within the { } of the class.

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    void study() {
        age = 20;  // inside the class ✅
    }
};

study() is inside Student, so it can access the members of Student.

2. Outside the class

Code written outside the class definition.

class Student {
public:
    int age;
};

int main() {
    Student s;
    s.age = 20;  // outside the class
}

main() is outside the Student class.

3. Child class

A child class (also called a derived class) is a class that inherits from another class.

class Student {
public:
    int age;
};

class CollegeStudent : public Student {
};

Here:

Student → parent/base class
CollegeStudent → child/derived class

We'll study inheritance properly later, so for now just remember what a child class means.

Now the access-specifier table makes sense:

| Access      | Inside class  | Outside class   | Child class |
| ----------- | ------------  | -------------   | ----------- |
| `public`    | ✅            | ✅             | ✅           |
| `private`   | ✅            | ❌             | ❌           |
| `protected` | ✅            | ❌             | ✅           |


private
class Student {
private:
    int age;

    void show() {
        age = 20;   // ✅ private can access private
    }
};

But outside:

int main() {
    Student s;

    s.age = 20;     // ❌
    s.show();       // ❌
}

private can only be accessed inside the same class.

protected
class Student {
protected:
    int age;
};

class Child : public Student {
    void show() {
        age = 20;   // ✅ protected can be accessed in child
    }
};

But outside:

int main() {
    Child c;

    c.age = 20;     // ❌
}

So without using public:

private:
Student class → ✅
Child class   → ❌
main/outside  → ❌

protected:
Student class → ✅
Child class   → ✅
main/outside  → ❌

That's the actual difference.

ok now with public

Yep. Now with public, you can access the members directly from outside the class.

private with public function
class Student {
private:
    int age;

public:
    void show() {
        age = 20;   // ✅ private accessed inside class
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s;

    s.show();       // ✅ public function can be called outside
    // s.age = 20;  // ❌ still cannot directly access private
}

The public function acts as the way to access/use the private member from outside.

protected with public function in child
class Student {
protected:
    int age;
};

class Child : public Student {
public:
    void show() {
        age = 20;   // ✅ protected accessed inside child
    }
};

int main() {
    Child c;

    c.show();       // ✅ public function can be called outside
    // c.age = 20;  // ❌ protected still cannot be directly accessed
}
Important

Making the function public does not make age public.

private age
    ↓
public function
    ↓
outside can call function

So the outside world accesses the private/protected data through a public interface, while the actual variable remains protected by the access specifier.


#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    void inside() {
        age = 20;        // ✅ inside same class
    }
};

class Child : public Student {
public:
    void child() {
        age = 21;        // ✅ inside child class
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s;

    s.age = 22;          // ✅ outside class

    Child c;
    c.age = 23;          // ✅ outside class through child object
}

So the easiest rule:

public → everywhere
protected → class + child
private → class only